    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d515">

                <pb facs="0710" n="620" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg620"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">300.115/14401: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-10-10T12:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 10,
                                1918—12 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 11—6:21
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Department’s 4th, mine, September 28, 12 noon. … Proposal extends only to goods
                    or proceeds on list. Status of remittances different <pb facs="0711" n="621"
                        xml:id="pg621"/> from goods in that title thereto has passed to enemy
                    addressees. This status described in my despatch 6719 dated September 5th, 1918,
                    subject George Dohrmann.<anchor n="20" xml:id="d515-f1"/> Am endeavoring to
                    arrange with British authorities for return to American claimants of the
                    deposits in Prize Court equivalent to total invoice value of the goods in
                    whatever goods were not contraband and title thereto can be proven to be in
                    American claimants.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
